When the program starts: JVM calls main(). main() executes the statement addition(). The addition() method is called. A stack frame is created for addition(). Control moves to the body of addition(). The statements are executed and Sum is 30 is printed. Control returns to main() after addition() finishes.

Answer: Writing a method means defining the task that the method should perform. Calling a method means requesting the program to execute that method. For example:

```java
public static void addition()
{
int a = 10;
int b = 20;
int sum = a + b;
System.out.println("Sum is " + sum);
}
```

This defines the method.

```java
addition();
```

This calls the method. A method can be written without being executed. It executes only when it is called.

Answer: Multiple methods can be defined separately and called from main() according to the required execution order.

```java
class Demo
{
public static void addition()
{
int a = 10;
int b = 20;
int sum = a + b;
System.out.println("Sum is " + sum);
}
public static void multiply()
{
int a = 10;
int b = 5;
int product = a * b;
System.out.println("Product is " + product);
}
public static void main(String[] args)
{
addition();
multiply();
}
}
```

Output: Sum is 30 Product is 50 The execution first goes to addition(), returns to main(), and then goes to multiply().

Answer: The multiply() method contains the statements required to perform multiplication.

```java
public static void multiply()
{
int a = 10;
int b = 5;
int product = a * b;
System.out.println("Product is " + product);
}
```

The method performs 10 * 5 and prints: Product is 50 Different tasks can therefore be separated into different methods.

